<h1>README for ISFDB Download Files</h1>
<hr>

As described elsewhere, one of the main inspirations for the ISFDB was the
IMDB, or Internet Movie Database. While the ISFDB has been fairly successful
in integrating several distinct services into a single cohesive webpage, it
has not been successful in fostering the kind of virtual community that helped
the IMBD to sustain its explosive growth. The IMDB started out as a University
site, with large amounts of diskspace and the ability to administer itself. 
The ISFDB started as a homepage at an ISP, with a maximum of 10 Meg of diskspace,
working under a Draconian Web administration setup. These differences inhibited
the development of a team approach to the ISFDB.  Now that the ISFDB is hosted
at SF Site, this is no longer the case - more resources are made available to the
ISFDB at SF Site. 
<p>
It has been painfully obvious over the past few months that the main bottleneck
for continued growth of the ISFDB is myself. I have fewer hours available to
maintain the ISFDB than I did a year ago. So, in order to keep the train rolling,
I am making available the source code and data files which make up the ISFDB.
Hopefully, people will become familiar with the ISFDB and begin to
construct a methodology for distributing the workload among more volunteers.
<p>
This file describes how to obtain the source code and data files to the ISFDB.
It also describes how to build and maintain a private copy of the database.
The following warnings should be noted before trying to install and run the 
ISFDB locally:

<ol>
  <li> Note that all of the source code is intended for UNIX systems; there is 
       absolutely no support for Windows or MacOS.
  <p>
  <li> A certain level of familiarity with software development is assumed - you
       should be familiar with UNIX, compilers, and makefiles before attempting
       to port and run this software on your system.
  <p>
  <li> The ISFDB doesn't use any advanced system calls or UNIX features - it's mostly
       string processing, so porting it to new platforms should require little more
       than finding the appropriate include files.  If you would like to see permanent 
       ifdef's to support your particular UNIX platform, just send them to me and I'll
       put them in if they are sensible.
  <p>
  <li> Compiling the ISFDB source code is only half of the installation problem -
       you must also set up your http server correctly. This means you'll need 
       administrative access to your web server, or at least cooperation from your web
       server administrator to set up a private copy of the ISFDB. Most ISPs have
       limitations on how CGI binaries are accessed. Almost all problems encountered
       with the ISFDB have been http configuration problems. I implore you not to 
       ask me how to fix http configuration problems - the details are almost certainly
       dependent upon the specific http daemon you are running, and I am only familiar
       with the NCSA server.
  <p>
  <li> If you use your version of the ISFDB to enter data locally via the user
       submission forms, I will be able to take the data that shows up in your
       copy of the NEWDATA file (that's where submissions go) and merge that into
       the ISFDB using current tools. There is, however, no facility in place for
       the merging of ISFDB files. That is, if you directly modify NOVELS, or
       SHORTFICTION, or any other data files, it is highly doubtful that I can diff
       your entries with the master copies, since they change on a daily basis.
       All merging tools to date assume that there is one, and only one, copy of the
       various ISFDB files.
</ol>

