<h2>The Available Filesets</h2>
The following filesets are available at 
<a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb">cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb</a>:

<ol>
  <li><a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/README.html">README.html</a>
  - A copy of the file you are reading.
  <li><a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/frame.tar.Z">frame.tar.Z</a>
  - This image contains the basic directory structure layout, as well as the top-level
  makefiles. You'll need this if you want to compile the database tools or do your own
  database compiles.  This file requires about 16K of space after expansion and extraction.
  <li><a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/data.tar.Z">data.tar.Z</a>
  - This image contains an uncompiled image of the ISFDB database data files. These are
  in the ISFDB data format specified in the FAQ. This file requires about 16Mbytes of space 
  after expansion.
  <li><a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/tools.tar.Z">tools.tar.Z</a>
  - This image contains the source code to the tools needed to compile the data files
  into an online database. There are also a number of utilities here used to find errors
  in the database, or in other databases that are being merged into the ISFDB.
  This file requires about 600K of space after expansion and extraction.
  <li><a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/src.tar.Z">src.tar.Z</a>
  - This image contains the source code to the online database applications. These
  will compile into the binaries that you are used to seeing in ISFDB URL's:
  exact_author.cgi, search.cgi, dataentry.cgi, etc... This file requires about 600K 
  of space after expansion and extraction.
  <li><a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/cmpl.tar.Z">cmpl.tar.Z</a>
  - This image contains a compiled image of the database data files. These files
  are used by the online database applications. If you have the tools fileset, you
  can generate these files yourself. This file requires about 15Mbytes of space after 
  expansion and extraction.
  <li><a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/html.tar.Z">html.tar.Z</a>
  - This image contains the ISFDB's html files. This file requires about 2.3Mbytes of
  space after expansion and extraction.
</ol>

<hr>
<h2>Which Filesets You Should Download</h2>
There are 3 different levels of interest you might have in these filesets.
<ol>
  <li> You want the whole shibang to make a private copy of the ISFDB.
  <li> You really only want the data, but don't feel like writing a parser from scratch. So you'd
  like to pick up some tools to get started with. 
  <li> You just want the ISFDB raw data, and are willing to write your own tools to
  translate the data into some other format useful to you. This is the simpliest -
  just download 
  <a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/data.tar.Z">data.tar.Z</a>
  and you're done.
</ol>

<hr>
<h2>Setup for the Entire ISFDB</h2>
The recommended approach to installing and porting the ISFDB to your UNIX system is:

<ol>
   <li> Download 
  <a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/frame.tar.Z">frame.tar.Z</a>, unzip and untar. 
  There are two top-level makefiles: <i>Makefile</i> and <i>dbase.mk</i>. You shouldn't have to alter
  <i>dbase.mk</i> at all. In <i>Makefile</i>, there are 8 defines which may need modification:
<p>
<ol>
  <li> <b>CC</b> - This should be changed to point to you compiler. The ISFDB uses ANSI
  prototyping (well, usually), so you need an ANSI-compilant C compiler.
  <li> <b>CFLAGS</b> - At a minimum you should define <b>-O</b> to optimize. Other defines
  which can be used here are:
  <ol>
     <li> <b>-DAIX</b> - Use this if your system is AIX.
     <li> <b>-DSUNOS</b> - Use this if your system is SunOS.
     <li> <b>-DLINUX</b> - Use this if your system is LINUX. This is a good starting
     point for any other OS as well.
     <li> <b>-DCHDIR</b> - This define should be enabled if your http server
     does not do a chdir to the working directory of the CGI binaries. You'll
     know you need this if an application can't find the database files.
     <li> <b>-DPOST_METHOD</b> - This is a legacy define. Leave it alone.
  </ol>
  <li> <b>LDFLAGS</b> - Use this if your compiler needs special linker options.
  <li> <b>HTMLDIR</b> - This should be set to the pathname of the local directory where
  the html files will reside. It is used to install the html and gif files. 
  <li> <b>HTMLLOC</b> - This should be set to the base directory of the html files that
  outside users see in URL's. For instance, the URL for the ISFDB at sfsite is
  <i>http://www.sfsite.com/isfdb/index.html</i>. So <i>HTMLLOC</i> would be set
  to <i>www.sfsite.com\/isfdb</i>. This define is used by the database applications
  to generate URL's on the fly.  Note that this define is passed to the compiler, so
  the "\/" notation is required. Also note that the leading <i>http://</i> should <b>not</b>
  be included in the define. This define is used by the ISFDB binaries to generate
  internal URLs, such as help files.
  <li> <b>CGIBIN</b> - This should be set to the pathname of the local directory where the
  cgi binaries will reside. It should be in a directory that your http server allows cgi
  access to. This define is used to intall the ISFDB binaries.
  <li> <b>DATABASE</b> - This is similar to <i>HTMLLOC</i>. This define should be set to
  the base path of the cgi binaries that outside users see in URL's. For instance, the URL
  to display an author's bibliography at sfsite is 
  <i>http://www.sfsite.com/isfdb-bin/exact_author.cgi</i>.
  So <i>DATABASE</i> would be set
  to <i>www.sfsite.com\/isfdb-bin</i>. This define is used to install the html files.
  The string <i>DBASELOC</i> will be replaced in each html file with the value of <i>DATABASE</i>.
  Note that this define is passed to the compiler, so the "\/" notation is required.
  <li> <b>HTFAKE</b> - This is nearly identical to <i>DATABASE</i>, except the define requires 
  a leading "/" for historical reasons.
</ol>
<p>
   <li> Download 
  <a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/src.tar.Z">src.tar.Z</a>, unzip and untar. 
  Type <i>make</i> at the top-level makefile. Do <b>not</b> cd to the src directory, as the
  makefile in src requires defines passed down from above. Port as necessary. When finished,
  the online application binaries should be built.
  <p>
   <li> Download 
  <a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/cmpl.tar.Z">cmpl.tar.Z</a>, unzip and untar. 
  This will place the pre-compiled database data files into a directory named <i>dbase.compiled</i>.
  To spot test your application binaries, cd to dbase.compiled, and try the following commands:
  <ol>
     <p>
     <li> <i>../bin/exact_author.cgi dan_simmons</i>. This should output a bunch of stuff
     which looks like an htmlized bibliography.
     <li> <i>../bin/AwYear.cgi Hu1996</i>. This should output a bunch of stuff
     which looks like an htmlized award listing for the Hugos for the year 1996.
     <li> <i>../bin/plist.cgi TLSTDGV19XX</i>. This should output a bunch of stuff
     which looks like an htmlized content listing of <i>The Last Dangerous Visions</i>.
     <p>
  </ol>
  If these work correctly, chances are that things will mostly work after the installation.
  <p>
   <li> Download 
  <a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/html.tar.Z">html.tar.Z</a>, unzip and untar. 
  A boatload of html files will be placed in a directory called <i>htmls</i>. The files
  will be in all caps, with the extension HTML. When the installation is made, a script
  called <i>make_htmls</i> will replace certain keywords in the files with the definitions
  in the top-level makefile. The modified files will be in lowercase.html files. The original
  files are never modified.
  <p>
  <li> Type <i>make install</i>. This should install the cgi binaries, html files, and database
  files into the appropriate directories. At this point, if your http server is configured
  correctly and you've ported everything correctly, you should have an ISFDB mirror up
  and running.
</ol>
<p>
In the future, when you wish to update the database image, you only need to download
<i>cmpl.tar.Z</i> and <i>html.tar.Z</i>. Unzip, and untar these files, and type
<i>make install</i>, and you will be up to date. Occassionally, changes will be made
to the source code, in which case you'll need to download <i>src.tar.Z</i>, unzip and
untar, and again type <i>make install</i>. In either case, you won't have to ever
modify the top-level makefiles.
<p>

<hr>
<h2>Setup for Data Files and Tools</h2>
If you want to play with compiling your own version of the database files from
the database source files, or if you want to start modifying the tools, you need to:
<p>
<ol>
   <li> Download 
  <a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/tools.tar.Z">frame.tar.Z</a>, unzip and untar. 
  This will create a directory structure for you, as give you some makefiles to work with.
  <a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/tools.tar.Z">tools.tar.Z</a>, unzip and untar. 
  This will place some source code files into a directory called <i>tools</i>. These will
  be compiled into binaries, which in turn will compile the database data files. To compile,
  type <i>make</i> from the top-level directory. Note that some files are shared between
  the tools and src directories (sfdbase.h, parse.c, print.c and sort.c). The master copies 
  live in the tools directory; links are made to the src directory when the tools are made.
  <p>
  <li> Download 
  <a href="ftp://cyberhome.cyberus.ca/pub/users/isfdb/data.tar.Z">data.tar.Z</a>, unzip and untar. 
  This will place data files into a directory called <i>dbase.ascii</i>. These are the raw
  datafiles of the ISFDB. These are the files which are edited when changes are made.
  <p>
  <li> Type <i>make dbase</i>. This will begin a highly-orchestrated compile procedure
  defined by <i>tools/ccdbase.c</i>. When complete, the compiled database will reside in
  <i>dbase.compiled</i>, while html files which are a side-effect of the compile will
  reside in <i>htmls</i>.
</ol>

